# core/view-contents-semantic - Which elements are placed on this diagram, without the views graph?
#
# Answers: the same question core/view-contents answers, from the other of the two
# statements converter output makes about view membership. Reads the one-hop
# arch:inView edge in the SEMANTIC graph instead of walking a node in the views
# graph.
#
# Does NOT prove: anything core/view-contents does not. What a view contains is
# still an editorial choice by whoever drew it, so "not on the diagram" is not
# "not in the architecture".
#
# WHEN TO USE THIS RATHER THAN core/view-contents. Prefer core/view-contents where
# the views graph exists: it is the richer route, it carries geometry, and it
# distinguishes two drawings of one element. This one exists for the case that route
# cannot serve — a graph published with `--views-profile no-views` or `no-diagrams`
# has no views graph at all, and the arch:View resources survive in the semantic
# graph while every node placing something on them is gone. core/view-contents then
# returns zero rows, which reads as "this diagram is empty" rather than "the evidence
# was dropped at publication".
#
# Requires roles.in_view, so it is refused against output converted before core
# 0.4.0, which emitted no such edge. That refusal is the point: the two templates
# cover different datasets and the profile decides which is offered.
#
# ONE GRAPH, not two. core/view-contents spans views and semantic, because the node
# is in one and the element's own facts are in the other — and getting that pairing
# backwards silently returns nothing. Here the edge and the facts are both semantic,
# so there is no boundary to get wrong.
#
# `?element a {{ROLE:element_class}}` is load-bearing, not a formality. arch:inView
# has domain arch:ModelConcept, so a QualifiedRelationship drawn on the view carries
# it too — the C4 fixture has three elements and one relationship pointing at one
# view. Without this line the template would return the relationship as a fourth
# "element" and disagree with core/view-contents, which reaches elements only through
# archvis:archElement and never sees it. Use core/view-usage's inverse or a
# relationship template to ask what arrows a view draws.
#
# Types are concatenated rather than given a row each, and STR() around ?type is
# load-bearing for the same reason it is in core/view-contents: GROUP_CONCAT over
# IRIs is a type error, and an erroring aggregate yields an unbound result rather
# than failing, so every row would arrive with an empty types column while the row
# count still looked right.
#
# Get VIEW_IRI from core/views rather than constructing it.
#
# Parameters: VIEW_IRI, LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT ?element ?label (GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT STR(?type); separator=", ") AS ?types) ?nativeId
WHERE {
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:semantic}}
    VALUES ?view { {{VIEW_IRI}} }
    ?element {{ROLE:in_view}} ?view ;
             a {{ROLE:element_class}} ;
             a ?type .
    OPTIONAL { ?element {{PATH:label}} ?label }
    OPTIONAL { ?element {{PATH:native_id}} ?nativeId }
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
}
GROUP BY ?element ?label ?nativeId
ORDER BY ?label ?element
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
